p.src:
A geodesic is defined by parallel transport of its tangent: $u^\nu\nabla_\nu u^\mu=0,
  \qquad
  u^\mu=\frac{dx^\mu}{d\tau}.$ In affine coordinates this becomes $\frac{d^2x^\mu}{d\tau^2}
  +\Gamma^\mu_{\alpha\beta}
   \frac{dx^\alpha}{d\tau}
   \frac{dx^\beta}{d\tau}=0.$ The variational description is local. A timelike geodesic locally maximizes proper time among nearby timelike curves, up to conjugate points. A Riemannian geodesic is a stationary curve. A sufficiently short segment locally minimizes length, but a longer segment may lose that property after conjugate points and become a saddle; it need not be the global shortest route.

