p.src:
The Euler–Lagrange equations derived with $t$ are equivalent to the geodesic equation, but $t$ is generally not an affine parameter. Changing from $\tau$ to $t$ uses $\frac{d}{d\tau}
  =\frac{dt}{d\tau}\frac{d}{dt}$ and introduces a term proportional to $dx^\mu/dt$: $\frac{d^2x^\mu}{dt^2}
  +\Gamma^\mu_{\alpha\beta}
  \frac{dx^\alpha}{dt}\frac{dx^\beta}{dt}
  =f(t)\frac{dx^\mu}{dt}.$ The function $f$ reflects the choice of a nonaffine parameter; it does not represent a new force.

